<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    What Android is allowed to copy off this phone.

    `android:allowBackup` defaults to true, which means the app's private
    storage — the saved Firebase Auth credential and the shared_preferences
    file — is included in Google's cloud backup and in device-to-device
    transfer. For an app whose local state authorises /api/appointments and
    /api/profile for a named patient at a mental-health clinic, that is a copy
    of someone's medical relationship sitting somewhere nobody decided to put
    it, and it is a disclosure the Play Data-safety form would then owe.

    Nothing here is worth backing up in any case: the appointments live on the
    server, and a patient reinstalling the app signs in again in ten seconds.

    Android 12 and newer read this file; older versions read
    `android:allowBackup="false"`, which is why both are set.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="root" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
